{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E1d9Qgm4qdJ2EvvqQJs8AK","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"atomic oxygen binding energy Cu(111) fcc site DFT PBE eV relative to gas-phase O atom\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914\"},{\"title\":\"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface\",\"url\":\"https://archive.aps.org/mar/2022/g47/5\"},{\"title\":\"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation\",\"url\":\"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476\"},{\"title\":\"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface\",\"url\":\"https://https-meetings--archive-aps-org-443.webvpn1.xju.edu.cn/mar/2022/g47/5/\"},{\"title\":\"s11433 012 4868 5\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"par.nsf.gov\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10320492\"},{\"title\":\"P 46 a11 6122919\",\"url\":\"https://accms.mobility.niche.tohoku.ac.jp/ACCMS-11/POSTER/P-46_a11_6122919.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"PhysRevB 73 165424\",\"url\":\"https://condensed.physics.usyd.edu.au/old_website/papers_aloy/PhysRevB_73_165424.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0511679v2\"},{\"title\":\"lian17 111101\",\"url\":\"https://www.henkelmanlab.org/pubs/lian17_111101.pdf\"}]\n\nThe best match for your exact setup is an arXiv preprint that uses PBE in VASP and defines binding energy relative to an isolated gas-phase O atom. It reports Oxygen (O*) fcc -5.000; hcp -4.905 in eV, and identifies the threefold hollow sites, both fcc and hcp, as the energetically preferred binding sites for O*. Its definition subtracts the energy of the isolated gas-phase O atom, so the sign convention is negative for favorable binding.\n\nOther sources give different numbers:\n\n- A conference poster using the Cu(111) slab reports adsorption energies of -6.37 eV and -6.26 eV for the fcc and hcp hollow sites. The snippet does not state the functional or the energy reference, so I can't confirm it matches your PBE/gas-atom setup.\n- A PhysRevB paper (73, 165424) uses the opposite sign convention, where a positive number indicates that the adsorption is exothermic. Its numerical values for O on Cu(111) did not appear in the results.\n- A 2013 Chinese Science paper concludes that the fcc-hollow site is the most stable site for oxygen adsorption, but the snippet gives no energy values.\n\nThe fcc-over-hcp preference is consistent across sources. The magnitude varies (about -5.0 eV versus about -6.3 eV), so check the reference state and functional before comparing numbers. I didn't find the full text of the PhysRevB paper or the 2013 paper in these results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8710ebc5-aae4-43a6-bdd3-f9c24a6e587f","uuid":"de35437c-ce92-4cb9-ac9d-dae906855f7d","timestamp":"2026-11-01T03:06:49.276Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"atomic oxygen binding energy Cu(111) fcc site DFT PBE eV relative to gas-phase O atom","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_014JPS3VFh5RmyxJpTfMMxzi","content":[{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914"},{"title":"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface","url":"https://archive.aps.org/mar/2022/g47/5"},{"title":"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation","url":"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476"},{"title":"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface","url":"https://https-meetings--archive-aps-org-443.webvpn1.xju.edu.cn/mar/2022/g47/5/"},{"title":"s11433 012 4868 5","url":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf"},{"title":"par.nsf.gov","url":"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10320492"},{"title":"P 46 a11 6122919","url":"https://accms.mobility.niche.tohoku.ac.jp/ACCMS-11/POSTER/P-46_a11_6122919.pdf"},{"title":"PhysRevB 73 165424","url":"https://condensed.physics.usyd.edu.au/old_website/papers_aloy/PhysRevB_73_165424.pdf"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0511679v2"},{"title":"lian17 111101","url":"https://www.henkelmanlab.org/pubs/lian17_111101.pdf"}]},"The best match for your exact setup is an arXiv preprint that uses PBE in VASP and defines binding energy relative to an isolated gas-phase O atom. It reports Oxygen (O*) fcc -5.000; hcp -4.905 in eV, and identifies the threefold hollow sites, both fcc and hcp, as the energetically preferred binding sites for O*. Its definition subtracts the energy of the isolated gas-phase O atom, so the sign convention is negative for favorable binding.\n\nOther sources give different numbers:\n\n- A conference poster using the Cu(111) slab reports adsorption energies of -6.37 eV and -6.26 eV for the fcc and hcp hollow sites. The snippet does not state the functional or the energy reference, so I can't confirm it matches your PBE/gas-atom setup.\n- A PhysRevB paper (73, 165424) uses the opposite sign convention, where a positive number indicates that the adsorption is exothermic. Its numerical values for O on Cu(111) did not appear in the results.\n- A 2013 Chinese Science paper concludes that the fcc-hollow site is the most stable site for oxygen adsorption, but the snippet gives no energy values.\n\nThe fcc-over-hcp preference is consistent across sources. The magnitude varies (about -5.0 eV versus about -6.3 eV), so check the reference state and functional before comparing numbers. I didn't find the full text of the PhysRevB paper or the 2013 paper in these results."],"durationSeconds":7.094151206999988,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":