{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgbUEMcUUm5kFrf3r4tjZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I posted my check (`77126e77-ed63-42d0-a0b2-37ed73e7a364`) as a `second_mlip` check with verdict **agree**. The worker's −4.84 eV holds up as a PBE-level number, but its exact value depends on how the model defines the energy of an isolated O atom.\n\n**Reproduction:** I reran the worker's MACE-MPA-0 setup independently and got the same numbers to within 0.001 eV. Against an isolated O atom, the fcc site gives −4.837 eV (3×3 slab) and −4.845 eV (4×4 slab), hcp gives −4.78 eV and top gives −3.08 eV.\n\n**Second model:** MACE-MP-0 medium, my recomputed value of −4.44 eV.\n- Measured against ½ O₂ instead of the O atom, the two models agree within about 0.03 eV (−1.67 vs −1.70 eV). So the surface side of the calculation is solid.\n- Measured against the O atom, they differ by 0.40 eV. All of that difference comes from the isolated-O energy, which in these models is a fitted constant (−1.548 vs −2.081 eV), not a calculated atom. Within an MLIP, the task's isolated-atom reference is set by that constant. The worker flagged this correctly.\n- MPA-0's O₂ binding energy (6.29 eV) matches DFT's (PBE's) usual ~1 eV overbinding; MP-0's (5.53 eV) does not. So MPA-0's atom energy is the more DFT-consistent of the two. Taking ½ O₂ ≈ −1.68 eV and adding half a DFT-like O₂ binding (~3.1 eV) gives about −4.8 eV, consistent with −4.84.\n- Both models put fcc as the most stable site, but in MP-0 it is only 0.013 eV below hcp.\n\n**Literature:** a search summary of a DFT preprint (arXiv:2505.12914) gives fcc −5.00 and hcp −4.905 eV against the O atom. I did not read the paper, so this is a secondary check. It supports the size of the number, the fcc-before-hcp order, and suggests the models may underbind by about 0.1–0.15 eV. A conference poster gives about −6.3 eV but doesn't state its reference or functional, so I discounted it. The calibration file has no adsorption data, so I couldn't compare against it.\n\n**Caveats in the note:** I ran no DFT, so a bias shared by both models of about 0.1–0.2 eV can't be ruled out. The number is atom-referenced and DFT-level. An experiment-scale value would be about 0.5 eV weaker (~−4.3 eV), and zero-point energy is not included.\n\nSources:\n- [arXiv:2505.12914 (PBE/VASP O on Cu(111))](https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914)\n- [ACCMS-11 poster P-46](https://accms.mobility.niche.tohoku.ac.jp/ACCMS-11/POSTER/P-46_a11_6122919.pdf)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2267,"cache_read_input_tokens":27970,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2267,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":829,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8710ebc5-aae4-43a6-bdd3-f9c24a6e587f","uuid":"3322c786-aca8-4566-b6c1-d8552b517537","timestamp":"2026-11-01T03:07:19.730Z","request_id":"req_011CgbUEMAvveDBMnNeVgF7N"}
